  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Reluctant Scaly Cricket (Cycloptilum pigrum) is a small, cryptic cricket belonging to the Scaly Crickets and Allies family, characterized by its reduced wings and ground-dwelling habits. Found throughout New England, this species is adapted to cool, moist forest environments and is rarely observed due to its secretive nature.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Heavily scaled appearance",
      "description": "Distinctive overlapping scales cover the body, giving it a textured, armor-like surface",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Reduced wings",
      "description": "Wings are vestigial or nearly absent, appearing as small pads rather than functional flight wings",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Small body size",
      "description": "Typically 8-12 mm in length with a compact, robust body shape",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "This species prefers moist leaf litter, decaying wood, and the forest floor in deciduous and mixed hardwood forests throughout New England.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The name 'Reluctant' refers to this cricket's extremely shy behavior—it rarely calls or exhibits the conspicuous singing displays common in other cricket species, making it difficult for researchers to locate and study in the field.",
